---
// Ported from the mkdocs about.md. Em-dashes cleaned; lily-background motif kept.
import BlogBaseLayout from '../layouts/BlogBaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'About — The Forge';
const description =
  'digitalgrease: cybersecurity engineer, hacker, maker. Where the digital and analog halves meet.';
---

<BlogBaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <article class="post">
    <header class="post-hero">
      <h1>About The Forge</h1>
    </header>

    <div class="post-body">
      <p>
        I'm digitalgrease. Cybersecurity engineer, hacker, maker. I grew up rural and
        poor with an internet connection and too much curiosity, became a father in my
        teens, and spent over a decade in cities chasing the version of opportunity
        that density promises. I've built a career in security and software engineering
        (vulnerability management, infrastructure, architecture), the kind of work
        where patience and ingenuity matter more than speed.
      </p>
      <p>
        A couple years ago, my partner and I moved back to rural land. The forge
        started glowing not long after.
      </p>
      <p>This blog is where both halves meet.</p>

      <hr />

      <h2>What You'll Find Here</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Digital:</strong> Security research, offensive tooling, engineering
        write-ups. The kind of work that lives in terminals and threat models. I build
        tools like <a href="https://apps.digitalgrease.dev/eve">EVE</a> (VoIP covert
        channels) and <a href="https://apps.digitalgrease.dev/fauxx">Fauxx</a>
        (ad-tech data poisoning) and write about them here, not just what they do, but
        how they think.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Analog:</strong> Blacksmithing, farming, building things with your
        hands. Walipini greenhouses, cedar enclosures, sheep and chickens. The work
        that happens after the screen goes dark and the forge starts to glow.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Prose:</strong> Essays on technology, culture, nostalgia, and the
        strange experience of watching the world rewire itself in real time. The kind
        of writing that happens when you sit with an idea long enough to find out what
        it actually means.
      </p>
      <p>
        Hot metal and code are more alike than people think. Both do exactly what you
        tell them, not what you mean. Both will expose a gap in your understanding
        immediately and without sympathy. The forge doesn't care about your intentions
        any more than the compiler does.
      </p>

      <p>
        The tools and apps themselves live at
        <a href="https://apps.digitalgrease.dev">apps.digitalgrease.dev ↗</a>.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <div class="forge-lily-bg">
        <img class="forge-lily-bg-img" src="/img/spiderlily.png" alt="" aria-hidden="true" />
        <h2>The Shape of This Place</h2>
        <p>
          I'm drawn to the quiet places where systems reveal their character. A forge
          burning in the dark like the phosphor glow of an old CRT, both casting the
          same patient light on whatever truth you're trying to shape. This blog is a
          living notebook: experiments, half-finished ideas, problems solved the hard
          way, and the occasional breakthrough.
        </p>
        <p>If you want the full picture: <a href="https://digitalgrease.dev">digitalgrease.dev</a></p>
        <p><em>Memento mori. Act with purpose. Leave a clean signal in the noise.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <nav class="post-foot-nav">
      <a href="/">← Back to The Forge</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BlogBaseLayout>

<style>
  .post {
    max-width: var(--w-content);
    margin: 0 auto;
  }

  .post-hero {
    padding: 2rem 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .post-hero h1 { margin: 0; }

  .post-foot-nav {
    margin-top: 3rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  /* Spider-lily background motif (ported from forge-components.css). */
  .forge-lily-bg {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 -1.5rem;
    padding: 2rem 1.5rem;
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .forge-lily-bg-img {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    min-width: 100%;
    min-height: 100%;
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
    object-fit: cover;
    opacity: 0.22;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 0;
  }

  .forge-lily-bg > :is(h2, p) {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }
</style>
